namespace trion:
    use formal

    section "Nouns and verbs":
        A noun denotes an entity.
        A verb denotes a relation among entities.
        A term means a noun or a verb of a specification.
        Each term has exactly one meaning.
        A formal assertion means an atom that a verb forms from nouns.

        Each specification defines the nouns and the verbs of the specification.
        A defined term means a term that a definition defines.
        A described term means a term that a descriptive sentence relates to a term.
        A primitive term means a term that a primitive declaration names.
        A grounded term means a defined term, a described term, or a primitive term.
        An ungrounded term means a term that has no definition, no description, and no primitive declaration.

        [WORD-GROUNDED]
        If a normative sentence names an ungrounded term, then the trion processor shall reject the normative sentence.

        [WORD-NO-OUTSIDE-KNOWLEDGE]
        The trion processor shall derive the meaning of each term from the statements of the specification.

    section "How a term is grounded":
        A definition has the word "means" after the defined term.
        A primitive declaration means a declaration that accepts a term without a definition.
        The statements that name a primitive term determine the meaning of the primitive term.

        example DESCRIBED-TERMS:
            kind consistency
            input """
Each room has exactly one owner.
[A] The owner of a room may archive the room.
"""
            expect consistent
